{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>Pinchas son of Elazar son of Aharon the priest, etc.</b> Scripture employed all [these appellations] for an inclusive reason, for even though he was 'son of Elazar,' who took for himself one of the daughters of Putiel, from the seed of Yitro who fattened calves for idolatrous worship, he should have restrained himself from this deed lest the scorners of the generation say to him: a daughter of Yitro — who permitted her to your father? And how do you take zealot vengeance against the Midianite woman? And likewise your father's father fattened calves for idolatrous worship — so how do you take zealot vengeance against idolatrous worship? And he was 'son of Aharon the priest,' and they would say to him: your father's father, this one fattened calves for idolatrous worship, and he made the [golden] calf. And even so, he was not insistent upon his own honor. And Rashi explained that the tribes were disgracing him, etc. — it is possible that this is his intent, for because the tribes were disgracing him, the verse came and traced his lineage to say: see, for he is a pious man. Even though by virtue of his being son of Elazar son of Aharon they had grounds to disgrace him, none the less he entered into the thickness of the beam and did not worry about his honor. And furthermore the verse informed us that he was 'one who turns back wrath, son of one who turns back wrath' (Sanhedrin 82b); therefore the deed of his ancestors was held in his hand." "<b>when he was zealous for My zealotry among them.</b> It does not say 'My zealotry upon them' but rather 'among them' — this teaches concerning every sanctification of the Name that it must be among the children of Israel specifically, as it is written (Leviticus 22:32): ״וְנִקְדַּשְׁתִּי בְּתוֹךְ בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״. Therefore it says (Numbers 25:7): ״וַיָּקָם״ Pinchas ״מִתּוֹךְ הָעֵדָה״. And it informed us of the praise of Pinchas, that he surrendered his life for the sanctification of the Name, and even though he was within the assembly — a dangerous place because of the kinsmen of Zimri — none the less there he placed his life in his palm." "And by way of allusion he took a spear (רֹמַח) in his hand, for in this deed he placed all his two hundred forty-eight (רַמַ״ח) limbs in his palm. And he was zealous with two acts of zealotry — one against idolatrous worship and one against immorality. Therefore it is said, 'when he was zealous for My zealotry.' Or [alternatively]: one for the honor of HaShem blessed be He, and one for the honor of Moshe, for everyone who profanes the Name does not grant honor to his master — by a fortiori reasoning, if the Shechinah is not accounted as weighty in his eyes, his master all the more so. And so too [Zimri] brazened his face against him and said to him: a daughter of Yitro — who permitted her to you? And upon this Pinchas relied, that he rendered a halakhic ruling for practice before Moshe his master, for he said that his master was touched by the matter, lest they say to him: a daughter of Yitro — who permitted her to you? Therefore it is said: ״לָכֵן אֱמֹר הִנְנִי נוֹתֵן לוֹ אֶת בְּרִיתִי שָׁלוֹם״ — and it should have said, 'Therefore say to him'! Rather, its meaning is that the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moshe: Therefore say to all Israel, on your own behalf, 'I am giving him My covenant of peace,' so that Israel would not say that you are not at peace with Pinchas because he rendered a halakhic ruling in your presence. Therefore you shall make it known to the public and you shall say on your own account, 'I am giving him,' etc. — and 'I' (הִנְנִי) refers to Moshe. (See Sanhedrin 82b: the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moshe: you be first to extend him peace.) And corresponding to that which he was zealous for his Elohim, it says: ״וְהָיְתָה לּוֹ וּלְזַרְעוֹ אַחֲרָיו בְּרִית כְּהֻנַּת עוֹלָם תַּחַת אֲשֶׁר קִנֵּא לֵאלֹהָיו״ — and this reason does not refer to what is written, 'I am giving him,' etc., for that is his reward for having been zealous for Moshe, as stated above. And it says, ״וַיְכַפֵּר עַל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״, for he brought this wicked man to atonement for all Israel, and it was accounted to him like the offering of bulls and lambs. Therefore [it says elsewhere]: 'HaShem has sworn… you are a priest forever' — just as you began with the service of the priesthood."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {1 "I read קְרוֹבֵי זִמְרִי as 'kinsmen of Zimri'; the phrase could alternatively mean 'those close to Zimri,' i.e., his faction.", 2 "The final phrase 'נשבע ה׳ אתה כהן לעולם כאשר התחלת בשימוש כהונה' appears to paraphrase Psalm 110:4 without a formal citation marker; translated as it stands."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:43:15.773488473Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}